## RAII owning pointer
##
## ScopedPtr holds a raw pointer and deletes the pointed-to object in its destructor.
## Ownership can be transferred out via .release and taken in via .assign.
##
## This allows us to ensure the contained object's lifetime is automatically managed,
## while being able to move that object from one scope to another,
## having it automatically destroyed when its final scope ends.
##
## This is in contrast to ordinary "stack-like" objects, which will be destroyed when the scope in which they were created ends.
## It is also in contrast to manual @new and @delete, which require the programmer to remember to delete the object at the right time.
##
## Example usage: @ScopedPtr.assign @new ObjectType
@class ScopedPtr {
	## @ignore
	@private ptr=@nullptr

	## @ignore
	@private @method _delete_ptr {
		if [[ @this.ptr != @nullptr ]]; then
			@ScopedPtr* ptr=@this.ptr
			@delete @ptr
			# "Dirty-casting" to @ScopedPtr is a language hack,
			# but is perfectly in line with the Bash++ spec
			#
			# All destructors are virtual: even if the declared type
			# of the pointer is completely unrelated to the actual
			# type of the object, the correct destructor will be
			# deduced at runtime and called.
		fi
	}

	## Take ownership of a raw pointer, deleting the previously owned object if any.
	## @param value The raw pointer to take ownership of.
	## @outputs nothing
	@public @method assign value {
		@this._delete_ptr
		@this.ptr=$value
	}

	## Release ownership and echo the raw pointer, without deleting the object.
	## After this call, the ScopedPtr will no longer manage the lifetime of the object.
	## @outputs The raw pointer that was released, or @nullptr if empty
	@public @method release {
		echo @this.ptr
		@this.ptr=@nullptr
	}

	## Echo the raw pointer without releasing ownership.
	## The returned pointer is non-owning.
	## DO NOT pass it to another ScopedPtr.
	## If you want to transfer ownership, use .release instead.
	## @outputs The raw pointer, or @nullptr if empty
	@public @method view {
		echo @this.ptr
	}

	@destructor {
		@this._delete_ptr
	}
}
